CPython 3.14.7: pycore_time.h forward declares `struct timeval` only for
clang. It declares three functions taking `struct timeval *` without
including <sys/time.h>, so on a platform where nothing else has already
defined that type, the struct is declared in PROTOTYPE SCOPE -- a
distinct, incomplete type -- and Python/pytime.c fails to compile with
"conflicting types for _PyTime_FromTimeval".

Whether it happens depends on what a platform's other headers drag in.
It does here, against picolibc.

diff -ru a/Include/internal/pycore_time.h b/Include/internal/pycore_time.h
--- a/Include/internal/pycore_time.h	2026-09-22 15:25:54.031182373 -0600
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_time.h	2026-09-22 15:25:54.050846387 -0600
@@ -59,9 +59,16 @@
 
 #include "pycore_runtime_structs.h" // _PyTimeFraction
 
-#ifdef __clang__
+/*
+ * Forward declared for EVERY compiler, not only clang. This header
+ * declares functions taking `struct timeval *` without including
+ * <sys/time.h>; where nothing else has pulled that type in first, the
+ * struct is declared in PROTOTYPE SCOPE and is therefore a different
+ * type from the real one -- and the definitions in Python/pytime.c then
+ * fail to compile with "conflicting types". Which happens depends
+ * entirely on what the platform's other headers happen to include.
+ */
 struct timeval;
-#endif
 
 #define _SIZEOF_PYTIME_T 8
 
